# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your key.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and never committed. The npm scripts that can spend money
# load it via `node --env-file-if-exists=.env`, so no shell restart is needed --
# just create the file and run `npm run smoke`.
#
# Nothing in `npm test` reads this file. The test script is a bare `node --test`,
# so the key is never in the process during a test run. That is deliberate: it is
# one of four independent guards against a test suite spending money.
#
# Get a key at https://fal.ai/dashboard/keys
FAL_KEY=your-fal-key-here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe. Only `npm run web` reads these, and only for buying credit packs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TEST-MODE KEYS FIRST. A live secret key in this file is a live secret key on
# a laptop; `sk_test_` and a test-mode webhook secret prove the whole path
# without a real card ever existing.
#
# The card is entered on Stripe's hosted page, on Stripe's domain. This
# repository never sees a card number and has no field to type one into.
#
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is sent to api.stripe.com and to nowhere else -- the host is
# a constant in scripts/billing/stripe.mjs with no parameter that can move it,
# which is the fix for finding F3 of the security review applied in advance.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-stripe-test-key-here
#
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET is what proves a webhook delivery really came from
# Stripe. Without it every delivery is answered 503 and NOTHING is granted --
# deliberately, because a 200 would tell Stripe to stop retrying a payment this
# app failed to credit. `stripe listen` prints one:
#
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
#
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-webhook-signing-secret-here
#
# Where this app is reachable from, for the two urls Stripe redirects a buyer
# back to after checkout. NEVER derived from the request's Host header, because
# that is a value the caller chooses. Unset is fine locally: it falls back to
# the address the server bound to.
# TIMESTAMP_PUBLIC_URL=https://timestamp.example
#
# Whether `x-forwarded-proto` decides the Secure attribute on cookies. Leave it
# unset until this app runs behind a TLS-terminating proxy the deployment
# actually has -- the header is whatever the client typed unless such a proxy
# rewrote it. Setting it to 1 is the operator vouching for that proxy.
# TIMESTAMP_TRUST_PROXY=1
